#![TODO]
// TODO: closure body that combines if-let pattern matching with a template
// string in the same closure exposes a TIR-to-WIR local-index collision.
//
// At template-expansion time, `synthesis::template::expand_templates` walks
// the parent function body and descends into closure bodies using the
// PARENT function's `local_count` allocator. Closure bodies have their own
// `local_index` namespace (see the comment on `TirExprKind::Closure`'s
// `address_taken_locals` field), so any locals injected by the template
// expander (the `__r` String builder) collide with closure-internal
// indices that the resolver allocated for `let result`, `let e`, etc.
//
// Pattern lowering (`lower::pattern::PatternLowerer::lower_expr`) has the
// same shape: it descends into closures sharing the parent allocator,
// so its own `__pattern_temp_N` and irrefutable-binding `let`s also land
// in the parent index space.
//
// At runtime the bug manifests as `if let Err(e) = result` matching when
// the result is actually `Ok` (because `__variant_test(...)` reads an
// unrelated local) and the closure traps inside the panic call. At
// compile time it can also surface as a Wasm validation error
// ("type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found (ref $type)") when
// two locals at the same Wasm index disagree on type.
//
// The fix needs both passes (and any future allocator-using pass) to
// switch to a closure-scoped allocator when descending into a closure
// body. The closure body should provide its own `local_count` (currently
// reconstructed at hoist time in `lower::closure::collect_locals_from_block`).
// A naive "scan body for max local_index + 1" approach was attempted and
// reverted because the post-hoist closure func ended up with sparse
// `UNKNOWN`-typed filler locals and the variant-test reference still
// resolved to the wrong WIR local — the index collision is established
// at resolve time, not just at synthesis/lower time.
//
// When this is fixed, the workaround in
// `benchmark/sqlite_parse/sqlite_parse.wado` that uses
// `sqlite::parse(&sql).is_ok()` instead of `if let Err(e) = ...` can be
// reverted to the natural form.

use { Stdout, println } from "core:cli";

struct Err1 {
    message: String,
}

fn parse(input: &String) -> Result<i32, Err1> {
    if input.len() == 0 {
        return Result::<i32, Err1>::Err(Err1 { message: "empty" });
    }
    return Result::<i32, Err1>::Ok(input.len());
}

fn run_with<T>(f: fn() -> T) -> T {
    return f();
}

export fn run() with Stdout {
    let sql = "select 1";
    let _ = run_with(|| {
        let result = parse(&sql);
        if let Err(e) = result {
            panic(`Parse error: {e.message}`);
        }
    });
    println("ok");
}

__DATA__
{"stdout_contains": ["ok"]}
